        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 🌧️ What Is IMD Gridded Data — and Why Should You Care?\n",
        "\n",
        "If you're studying agriculture, food security, hydrology  or climate change science in India, **rainfall data is your foundation**. Every question — *Did the monsoon arrive late? Is drought risk increasing? How much water does the Kharif crop need?* all starts with good precipitation records.\n",
        "\n",
        "The **India Meteorological Department (IMD)** provides one of the most valuable publicly available climate datasets for India: **high-resolution gridded daily rainfall**. Here's what makes it special:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note icon=false}\n",
        "## 📌 Key Facts About IMD Gridded Rainfall\n",
        "\n",
        "| Feature | Details |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| **Spatial Resolution** | 0.25° × 0.25° (~28 km grid) |\n",
        "| **Temporal Resolution** | Daily |\n",
        "| **Coverage** | Entire India |\n",
        "| **Period Available** | 1901 – present |\n",
        "| **Data Type** | Rain gauge–interpolated gridded data |\n",
        "| **Citation** | Pai et al. (2014); Rajeevan et al. (2006) |\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "The data is derived by interpolating observations from **~6,950 rain gauge stations** across India into a regular grid. This means even if there's no station in your district, you still get a daily rainfall estimate for every 0.25° cell.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip icon=true}\n",
        "## 🌾 Why It Matters for Agricultural Research\n",
        "\n",
        "For crop science and agronomy students, this data unlocks:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Drought monitoring** : identify consecutive dry days during growing seasons\n",
        "- **Rainfall onset/withdrawal** : track monsoon onset for sowing decisions\n",
        "- **Excess rainfall events** : assess waterlogging risk in paddy fields\n",
        "- **Long-term trend analysis** : is rainfall becoming more erratic over 50+ years?\n",
        "- **Agro-climatic zone characterization** : classify regions by rainfall patterns\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 🗺️ Understanding the Data Format\n",
        "\n",
        "Before writing any code, let's understand what you're downloading.\n",
        "\n",
        "IMD provides data in a custom binary `.grd` format — **one file per year**. Each file contains a 3D array:\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "Shape: (365 or 366 days) × (latitudes) × (longitudes)\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "The full-India grid covers:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Latitude:** 6.5°N to 38.5°N (129 grid points)\n",
        "- **Longitude:** 66.5°E to 100.0°E (135 grid points)\n",
        "\n",
        "So for each year, you're getting: `365 × 129 × 135 = ~6.3 million values`.\n",
        "\n",
        "We use the [`imdlib`](https://github.com/iamsaswata/imdlib) Python library — built specifically for this format — to make downloading and reading these files effortless.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## 🐍 Setting Up Anaconda & JupyterLab\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the most important section if you're just starting out. **Anaconda** is the standard Python distribution for scientific computing — it bundles Python, a package manager (`conda`), and hundreds of scientific libraries in one installer. Think of it as your complete climate science workbench.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Why Anaconda — and Not Plain Python?\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note icon=false}\n",
        "## Anaconda vs Plain Python + pip\n",
        "\n",
        "| | Plain Python + pip | Anaconda + conda |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| **Package conflicts** | Common and frustrating | Rare — conda resolves dependencies properly |\n",
        "| **Scientific libraries** | Install each one manually | Most included out of the box |\n",
        "| **Environments** | Possible but fiddly | First-class, trivial to create |\n",
        "| **Geospatial tools** (GDAL, cartopy) | Often breaks on install | Works reliably via `conda-forge` |\n",
        "| **Best for** | Web/app developers | Scientists and researchers ✅ |\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 1 — Download the Installer\n",
        "\n",
        "You have two solid options:\n",
        "\n",
        "**Option A — Anaconda (full installer, ~800 MB)**  \n",
        "Includes Python, conda, JupyterLab, and 250+ scientific packages pre-installed.  \n",
        "Best if you want zero-friction setup and have disk space to spare.\n",
        "\n",
        "→ Download: [https://www.anaconda.com/download](https://www.anaconda.com/download)  \n",
        "Choose your OS (Windows / macOS / Linux).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Option B — Miniforge (minimal installer, ~90 MB)**  \n",
        "Lighter and faster. Defaults to the `conda-forge` channel which has more up-to-date climate packages. This is what the author uses.\n",
        "\n",
        "→ Download: [https://github.com/conda-forge/miniforge/releases/latest](https://github.com/conda-forge/miniforge/releases/latest)\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## 💡 Which Should You Choose?\n",
        "\n",
        "**New to Python / student?** → Go with **Anaconda (Option A)**. Larger download, but requires zero extra configuration.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Comfortable with terminals?** → Go with **Miniforge (Option B)**. Faster, leaner, and `conda-forge` packages are more current.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 2 — Install\n",
        "\n",
        "**On Windows:**\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Run the downloaded `.exe` installer\n",
        "2. Select **\"Just Me\"** (not \"All Users\")\n",
        "3. ✅ Check **\"Add Anaconda to my PATH\"** — essential for terminal access\n",
        "4. Click Install, wait ~5 minutes\n",
        "\n",
        "Open **Anaconda Prompt** from the Start menu to test it.\n",
        "\n",
        "**On Linux / macOS:**\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "# Make the installer executable, then run it\n",
        "chmod +x Anaconda3-*.sh      # or Miniforge3-*.sh\n",
        "bash Anaconda3-*.sh\n",
        "\n",
        "# Say YES when asked to initialise conda\n",
        "# Then restart your terminal (close and reopen)\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "**Verify it worked:**\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "conda --version\n",
        "# Expected: conda 24.x.x\n",
        "\n",
        "python --version\n",
        "# Expected: Python 3.12.x\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "If either command says \"not found\", the PATH wasn't set correctly — re-run the installer and check the PATH option.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 3 — Create a Dedicated `climate` Environment\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-warning}\n",
        "## ⚠️ Never Work in the `base` Environment\n",
        "\n",
        "The `base` environment is conda's default. Treat it as read-only. Installing packages directly into `base` causes version conflicts that are painful to debug.\n",
        "\n",
        "Always create a **named environment per project**. It takes 2 minutes and saves hours of frustration.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "# Create a fresh environment called 'climate' with Python 3.11\n",
        "# (3.11 has the best compatibility with climate packages as of 2025)\n",
        "conda create -n climate python=3.11 -y\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Now activate it:\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "conda activate climate\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Your terminal prompt changes from `(base)` to `(climate)` — this confirms which environment is active. **Always check this before installing or running anything.**\n",
        "\n",
        "To see all your environments at any time:\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "conda info --envs\n",
        "# The active one has a * next to it\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 4 — Install the Full Climate Stack\n",
        "\n",
        "With `(climate)` active, install everything in one command. We use `-c conda-forge` because conda-forge has more up-to-date and compatible builds of scientific packages than the default Anaconda channel:\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "conda install -c conda-forge \\\n",
        "  jupyter jupyterlab \\\n",
        "  numpy pandas \\\n",
        "  xarray netcdf4 h5netcdf \\\n",
        "  matplotlib cartopy \\\n",
        "  imdlib \\\n",
        "  ipykernel \\\n",
        "  -y\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This will take 3–8 minutes. Grab a coffee.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note icon=false}\n",
        "## 📦 What Each Package Does\n",
        "\n",
        "| Package | Role in This Workflow |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| `jupyterlab` | The interactive notebook interface we'll work in |\n",
        "| `numpy` | Fast numerical arrays — the backbone of everything |\n",
        "| `pandas` | Tabular data (used for CSV exports and time series) |\n",
        "| `xarray` | Labelled multi-dimensional arrays — our main data container |\n",
        "| `netcdf4` / `h5netcdf` | Engines to read/write NetCDF `.nc` files |\n",
        "| `matplotlib` | Plotting and figures |\n",
        "| `cartopy` | Geographic projections and map features |\n",
        "| `imdlib` | Read and download IMD `.grd` binary files |\n",
        "| `ipykernel` | Connects this environment as a kernel inside JupyterLab |\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "Verify everything installed correctly:\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "python -c \"import imdlib, xarray, cartopy, jupyterlab; print('✅ All packages ready')\"\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 5 — Register the Environment as a JupyterLab Kernel\n",
        "\n",
        "This one-time step makes the `climate` environment appear as a selectable kernel inside JupyterLab:\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "python -m ipykernel install --user --name climate --display-name \"Python (climate)\"\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "You only need to do this once. After this, every time you open JupyterLab you'll see **\"Python (climate)\"** as a kernel option.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Step 6 — Launch JupyterLab\n",
        "\n",
        "```bash\n",
        "# Step 1: activate the environment\n",
        "conda activate climate\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 2: navigate to your project folder\n",
        "cd ~/your-project-folder\n",
        "\n",
        "# Step 3: launch\n",
        "jupyter lab\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "JupyterLab will open automatically in your browser at `http://localhost:8888`.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## 🖥️ Quick JupyterLab Orientation\n",
        "\n",
        "When it opens you'll see:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Left sidebar** — file browser, running sessions, table of contents\n",
